DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     These utilities are user tools to manipulate the named extended at‐
     tributes on files and directories.  The _a_t_t_r_n_a_m_e_s_p_a_c_e argument should be
     the namespace of the attribute to retrieve: legal values are uusseerr and
     ssyysstteemm.  The _a_t_t_r_n_a_m_e argument should be the name of the attribute,
     _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e the name of the target file or directory, _a_t_t_r_v_a_l_u_e a string to
     store in the attribute.

     The following options are available:

     --ff      (Force.)  Ignore errors on individual filenames and continue with
             the remaining arguments.

     --hh      (No follow.)  If the file is a symbolic link, perform the opera‐
             tion on the link itself rather than the file that the link points
             to.

     --ii      (From stdin.)  Read attribute data from stdin instead of as an
             argument.

     --nn      (NUL-terminate.)  NUL-terminate the extent content written out.

     --qq      (Quiet.)  Do not print out the pathname and suppress error mes‐
             sages.  When given twice, print only the attribute value, with no
             trailing newline.

     --ss      (Stringify.)  Escape nonprinting characters and put quotes around
             the output.

     --xx      (Hex.)  Print the output in hexadecimal.

